# `interp::linear`

Evaluates the piecewise-linear interpolant of one or many curves at one or many query points.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series interp::linear(const pluss::Series &coordinate, const pluss::Series &values, const pluss::Series &query, interp::EdgeMode edge = interp::EdgeMode::Clamp);
```

## What It Does

`coordinate` is a strictly increasing one-dimensional grid of at least two points. `values` has shape `[..., n]`, the sample of each curve at the `n` grid points; every leading axis is a separate curve, so a matrix of samples is a batch of curves sharing one grid. `query` is a scalar or a one-dimensional `Series` of points. Each query is placed in its grid interval and the two bracketing samples are blended linearly. The result has shape `[...]` for a scalar query and `[..., m]` for `m` queries.

Outside the grid the behaviour is set by [`EdgeMode`](EdgeMode.md): clamp to the end value, extend the end segments linearly, or throw. Values may be real or complex (interpolated component-wise); `coordinate` and `query` are real floating `Series` of the same type and on the same platform as the values. The interval search and the gather run as `Series` operations, and the interpolant is built from differentiable operations, so a `Series` marked for differentiation receives the interpolation weights of the two samples that bracket each query.

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series &coordinate`
: A strictly increasing 1-D grid of at least two points.

`const pluss::Series &values`
: Samples with shape `[..., n]`; real or complex.

`const pluss::Series &query`
: A scalar or 1-D `Series` of points; the same floating type as `coordinate`.

`interp::EdgeMode edge`
: Behaviour outside the grid; default `Clamp`.

## Returns

A `Series` of shape `[...]` for a scalar query and `[..., m]` for `m` queries.

## Errors

- A coordinate that is not 1-D, strictly increasing and finite, with at least two points, throws (`std::invalid_argument`).
- `values` whose last axis is not the grid length, a query of rank above 1, mismatched dtypes or platforms, or non-finite data throws.
- With `EdgeMode::Error`, a query outside the grid throws (`std::out_of_range`).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

One curve at several queries. The samples `0, 10, 40, 30` at `x = 0, 1, 2, 4`: halfway between the first two is 5, a quarter of the way from 2 to 4 is 37.5.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0});
  const Series y = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0, 40.0, 30.0});

  out.writeln("values =", interp::linear(x, y, pluss::make_series({0.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

A scalar query returns a scalar.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0});
  const Series y = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0, 40.0, 30.0});

  out.writeln("y(3) =", interp::linear(x, y, pluss::make_series(3.0)).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

A batch of curves. Two curves on the same grid, read at the same two queries: the result is `[2, 2]`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0});
  const Series y = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0, 40.0, 0.0, -2.0, -4.0}, {2, 3});
  const Series r = interp::linear(x, y, pluss::make_series({0.5, 1.5}));
  out.writeln("shape =", r.sizes(), ", values =", r.reshape({4}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

Outside the grid. By default a query beyond the ends takes the end value; `Linear` extends the end segments; `Error` throws.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0});
  const Series y = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0, 40.0, 30.0});

  const Series outside = pluss::make_series({-1.0, 5.0});
  out.writeln("Clamp  :", interp::linear(x, y, outside).items<double>());
  out.writeln("Linear :", interp::linear(x, y, outside, interp::EdgeMode::Linear).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5

A gradient through the interpolant. At `x = 0.5` between samples 0 and 10, the derivative with respect to each sample is its weight, 0.5.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0});
  Series y = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0, 40.0});
  y.tracks_slope_(true);
  const Series v = interp::linear(x, y, pluss::make_series(0.5));
  v.reverse();
  out.writeln("value =", v.item<double>(), ", weights =", y.sensitivity().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 6

A bad grid is rejected: the coordinate must be strictly increasing.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace interp = pluss::subsp::math::interp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  try {
    (void)interp::linear(pluss::make_series({0.0, 2.0, 1.0}), pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0}), pluss::make_series({0.5}));
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

